<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">War of the Remences</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1462">1462</a>–<a href="/years/1486">1486</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Remença wars · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The War of the Remences was a series of peasant risings in fifteenth-century Catalonia by the remences, serfs who owed payments to redeem themselves from their lords. They fought alongside John II during the Catalan Civil War and rose again in 1484 under Pere Joan Sala. Ferdinand II settled the conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sentence-of-guadalupe">Sentence of Guadalupe</a> in 1486, which abolished the worst feudal abuses against payment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-the-remences.jpg" alt="War of the Remences" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Generalitat de Catalunya · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Primer_mapa_impr%C3%A8s_de_Catalunya_com_a_estat,_1602-1605,_Jan_Baptist_Vrients.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Catalan word remença comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> redementia and stresses the possibility of redemption from servitude. In the early 14th century the growth of the Catalan cities and the expansion of the Aragonese Empire drew people from the countryside, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> reduced the rural population further. The nobility responded by strictly enforcing the evil customs that tied peasants to the land, and seignorial rights in general, more harshly than in the centuries before.</p><p>The poorest peasants gave the strongest support to open rebellion, while the better-off, the majority on the Plain of Vic, in l'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emporda">Empordà</a> and in el Vallès, appealed to the king for reform. The crown, in a constant power struggle with the nobility, had reasons to favour the peasants. Alfonso V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> allowed them to form a sindicat remença, a peasants' <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>, and granted them their liberty, but the opposition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a> and of the Generalitat, controlled by the nobles, made him reverse himself. His successor John II sought the peasants' help, and by May 1461 they had declared for the king against the nobles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first rising, in 1462, coincided with the nobles' war against John II. Under Francesc de Verntallat the peasants fought mainly in the mountainous interior, while the king's forces fought nearer the coast. After ten years John won, but he neither abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a> nor brought significant reform.</p><p>A second rising began with the Mieres Uprising in the Valley of Mieres on 22 September 1484, led by Pere Joan Sala, once Verntallat's captain. Sala extended his power over the Muntanya, the Plain of Vic, Selva, Garrotxa and Gironès, but the peasants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emporda">Empordà</a> did not follow him, and his attempt on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a> on 14 December failed. The Infante Enric, the king's lieutenant, had to fall back before the size of his forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Faced with the rebellion, Ferdinand II issued the Sentencia de Guadalupe in 1486. It outlawed the more severe abuses and let remença households redeem themselves by a payment of 60 sous each, leaving a rural society still feudal in character but significantly reformed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “War of the Remences” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>War of the Remences</b> ended with Ferdinand II’s Sentence of Guadalupe of 1486, which let peasants buy their freedom from the evil customs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-catalonia">Principality of Catalonia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
